  "verbalization": "Undecylenic Acid appears to be linked to Tinea Corporis through a chain of indications involving Sulconazole and Econazole. The evidence suggests that Undecylenic Acid may treat Tinea Infection, which in turn is treated by Sulconazole, and both Sulconazole and Econazole are used to treat Tinea Corporis. This implies a potential pathway where Undecylenic Acid could influence the progression or treatment of Tinea Corporis through the use of these antifungal agents. The shared use of Sulconazole in both Tinea Pedis and Tinea Corporis further suggests a possible overlap in the mechanisms of action or therapeutic applications. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of antifungal agents in the treatment of tinea infections, with Undecylenic Acid potentially contributing to the management of Tinea Corporis through its interaction with these agents.",
